/* V91 — the seeded stream belongs to the simulation.

   ---- How this was found ----

   V90 needed one thing to be true: that the same seed deals the same field. Every part of this
   codebase that touches randomness says it already is. V39's header says the daily arena works
   "because everyone is playing the same field". V20 made spawns deterministic "so two duellists meet
   identical enemies". V76 and V77 both carry a warning in capital letters that `rng()` must never be
   called from drawing code because it advances `game.rng`, the stream the spawner draws from.

   It is not true, and it has not been true since the first commit.

   Three runs of solo on the identical seed, recording every arrival: the first fifteen matched, then
   the radius, speed and position of arrival sixteen drifted, and by arrival twenty-two the seeded
   cursor itself had gone somewhere else entirely — 3891741909 in one run against 3259906239 in
   another. So the field is the same for about twenty seconds and then quietly becomes a different
   game.

   Wrapping `rng()` in a census that logged its own stack and mapping the line numbers back to source
   named the culprits in one pass, and they are not in any of the layers that carry the warning.
   They are in the base:

     356 calls   03.part   `draw()` — `ctx.translate((rng()-.5)*game.shake, (rng()-.5)*game.shake)`
     320 calls   02.part   `explode()` — six per particle
     192 calls   01.part   `spawnOrb()` — the legitimate consumer
      64 calls   03a.part  spawn variants — also legitimate

   **The camera shake spends two of the simulation's random numbers on every rendered frame.** A run
   that renders at 60fps consumes twice the stream of the same run at 30fps, so the arena a player
   meets depends on their phone. And `explode` is worse than frame-coupled: its particle count is
   `quality.particles`, the adaptive quality setting, which is *itself* lowered when the framerate
   drops. The number of random numbers a death consumes is a function of how fast the device is
   running. Two players cannot share a field across that.

   ---- The fix ----

   Not a hunt for call sites. Chasing the four found today leaves the fifth to be written next month,
   and the three warnings already in this tree are proof that "remember not to do this" does not
   hold across ninety-four parts.

   Instead, one rule with a seam that enforces it: **the seeded stream is live inside `update` and
   nowhere else.** `rng()` reads and writes exactly one variable, `game.rng`, so the simulation
   cursor is swapped in on the way into `update` and swapped back out on the way through. Rendering,
   explosion particles, deferred timeouts, input handlers and every layer written after this one get
   a second, cosmetic stream that nobody depends on — and they get it without knowing this part
   exists, without changing a line inside the minified base, and without any of them being able to
   reach the spawner's stream by accident again.

   The three parts that solved this locally by reaching for `Math.random()` instead — V76, V77, V85 —
   were right, and are left exactly as they are. This makes their discipline the default rather than
   a thing each author has to rediscover.

   ---- What is deterministic afterwards, stated precisely ----

   Arrivals. Every hostile's type, position, radius, speed, elite roll and formation shape, and the
   order they arrive in, because every one of those is decided inside `update` from a cursor that now
   only advances when the simulation asks it to.

   Not the particles, not the shake, not the exact frame a wave turns over. Those are cosmetic, they
   were always going to vary with the device, and pretending otherwise is what produced a claim that
   three different headers repeated and nobody had measured.

   Re-measured after: three runs on one seed, twenty-six spawn calls each, every call identical in
   cursor-in, cursor-out, hostile type, x to three decimals, radius, elite roll and arrival time —
   0.12s, 0.45s, 0.78s, 1.22s, 1.78s, the same list in all three.

   One residual, stated rather than hidden. Fall speed lands within 0.004% rather than exactly:
   V21's difficulty ramp is `v21Stage()+game.elapsed/16` and `game.elapsed` is a float accumulating
   real frame times, so a hostile spawned at t=0.1234 and t=0.1237 gets 68.744 against 68.745. That
   is 0.002 pixels a second on a fifty-pixel-a-second fall, it is a rate rather than a threshold —
   nothing in the tree compares a hostile's speed to anything — and it cannot change a decision. It
   is left alone because quantising a clock to buy a fourth decimal nobody can perceive is the kind
   of fix that reads as rigour and is actually just risk. */

const V91={on:true};

/* The two cursors. `game.rng` always holds whichever one is currently live, because that is the only
   variable `rng()` knows about — the whole mechanism is keeping the other one in a local. */
let v91Sim=1;
let v91Cos=1;
let v91Depth=0;

function v91Fresh(){
 try{return crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint32Array(1))[0]|0;}
 catch(e){return (Math.random()*4294967296)|0;}
}

/* Outermost, so `game.rng` has already been set from the seed by the base and by every layer that
   adjusts it. From here the seeded value is the simulation's, and the world runs on a cosmetic
   stream that starts somewhere different every run — a replayed field should look alive, not
   reproduce the same sparks. */
const v91Begin=begin;
begin=function(mode='solo',seed){
 const out=v91Begin(mode,seed);
 if(V91.on){
  v91Sim=game.rng|0;
  v91Cos=v91Fresh();
  game.rng=v91Cos;
 }
 return out;
};

const v91Update=update;
update=function(dt){
 if(!V91.on)return v91Update(dt);
 /* Guarded rather than assumed non-reentrant: a nested call would otherwise swap the cosmetic cursor
    in as the simulation's on the way out, which is the one failure mode of this shape and the one
    that would be hardest to recognise later. */
 if(v91Depth++){try{return v91Update(dt);}finally{v91Depth--;}}
 v91Cos=game.rng|0;
 game.rng=v91Sim;
 try{
  return v91Update(dt);
 }finally{
  v91Sim=game.rng|0;
  game.rng=v91Cos;
  v91Depth--;
 }
};
